@page "/severity"

<PageTitle>Severity levels</PageTitle>
<h1>Severity levels</h1>
<p>Warnings and infos render distinctly and never block submission; only errors do.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Listing.cs", "SeverityLevels.razor", "SeverityLevels.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: required (error severity: blocks submission).</li>
            <li><strong>Description</strong>: an exclamation mark draws a <em>warning</em> (advisory: never blocks).</li>
            <li><strong>Tags</strong>: more than five draws an <em>info</em> (advisory: never blocks).</li>
            <li>A live pass records a verdict for every field you have engaged — here, every field a committed change has named — and for no others, even though validating any one of them runs the whole model to get there. Title starts required and empty, and nothing has ever engaged it: no live pass has recorded anything for it, so its error stays invisible until a submit checks every field at once.</li>
            <li>Advisory-only submissions still announce the summary — screen readers hear the warnings and infos that did not block.</li>
            <li>This page mounts <strong>two</strong> summaries rather than one: <code>Show="SummaryFilter.Errors"</code> lists only what blocks, and <code>Show="SummaryFilter.Advisories"</code> lists the warnings and infos that do not. A summary with nothing to show draws nothing — its wrapper and fixed-role live region stay in the page, empty, so the element a screen reader watches exists before the first issue ever lands in it. Count the panels when there is something to show, though, and you can see three: a summary bands its issues by severity and gives every band a panel of its own, so the advisory one draws Warnings above Info.</li>
            <li>Two summaries are two live regions, and an issue listed by both would be read twice — which is why these two filters are chosen to be disjoint rather than one of them being left unfiltered. The bands inside a summary are not live regions of their own: the <code>role</code> sits on the fixed-role region wrapping them all, so however many panels one of these summaries draws, a screen reader hears one announcement from it.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type <code>Great synth!</code> into Description and leave the field — the warning appears immediately, in amber.</li>
            <li>Enter six comma-separated tags and leave the field — the info appears, in purple-blue.</li>
            <li>With a warning and an info showing and Title still empty and untouched, look at the top of the form — the two panels standing there are the Warnings and Info bands of the advisory summary, and the error summary draws nothing: not because Title is valid, but because no live pass has ever recorded a verdict for it; only a submit will.</li>
            <li>Fill Title and submit — it goes through: the status line counts the warnings and infos that did NOT block.</li>
            <li>Clear Title and leave the field — clearing engages it, so the error arrives on that commit, before you reach the button. Submit anyway: only the error blocks, and the advisories keep their place and colour in the summary.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_listing">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary Show="SummaryFilter.Errors" ErrorsHeading="Errors" />
    </div>
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary Show="SummaryFilter.Advisories" WarningsHeading="Warnings" InfosHeading="Info" />
    </div>

    <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_listing.Title" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Title" /></div>
    <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_listing.Description" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Description" /></div>
    <div class="field"><label>Tags (comma-separated) <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_listing.Tags" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Tags" /></div>

    <div class="actions"><button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="Submit">Submit</button></div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
